==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|kneo|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*knewą}} (compare Low German {{term|Knee|lang=nds}}, Dutch {{term|knie|lang=nl}}, English {{term|knee|lang=en}}, Danish {{term|knæ|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|knä|lang=sv}}), from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*ǵnéwo-}}, a thematic derivative of {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*ǵónu}}. Compare {{etyl|hit|-}} {{term|𒄀𒉡|tr=genu|lang=hit|sc=Xsux}}, Latin {{m|la|genū}}, {{etyl|xto|-}} {{term|kanweṃ|lang=xto}} (dual), {{etyl|txb|-}} {{term|kenī|lang=txb}}, Ancient Greek {{term|γόνυ||knee|lang=grc}}, {{term|γωνία||corner, angle|lang=grc}}, Old Armenian {{term|ծունր||lang=xcl}}, Avestan {{term|𐬲𐬥𐬎𐬨||tr=žnum|lang=ae|sc=Avst}}, Sanskrit {{term|जानु|tr=jānu|lang=sa}}.

